  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "MIr3wTzXJfaYJVKs+sk7gHblxJo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:31.154Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Glintt's verticals \u2014 healthcare, pharmacy and retail \u2014 are among the most attractive in the pilot, and its tool field records the reason the row is still do-not-pitch: its platforms are its own healthtech products.\n\nThat combination is the recurring trap in this part of the dataset. Sector fit tells you the firm reaches the right buyers with the right process problems; business model tells you whether it can carry someone else's component to them. A product house monetises its own roadmap, so a component it adopted would compete for room against features it planned to build and sell itself.\n\nPinkRoccade sits in exactly the same position on the Dutch side of the corpus, with ideal healthcare and municipal verticals and a product rather than custom-build model. Two independent rows arriving at the same conclusion suggests the constraint is structural in health and public-sector software: the firms closest to those buyers tend to own the software, not integrate it. The row otherwise remains a mechanically rescored v2 entry with an OSS-portfolio screen outstanding.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Glintt (PT)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Healthcare, pharmacy, retail",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:31:31.154011Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:31.154014Z"
    }
  },
